<!--
  What iOS needs to be told before this app can talk to a till.

  These are merged into ios/App/App/Info.plist by build-ipa.js. They are kept
  here, in the repo, rather than edited into a generated project, because
  `npx cap add ios` regenerates that project and would silently drop them - and
  the symptom is not a build error. It is an app that installs, launches, and
  then cannot find the shop's server, which looks like a bug in the discovery
  code rather than a missing declaration.

  Two separate permissions, often confused:

  NSAppTransportSecurity
      iOS refuses plain http by default. The till is reached at
      http://192.168.x.x:5555 and holds no certificate, so an exception is
      required. NSAllowsLocalNetworking is the narrow one: it permits http to
      local addresses ONLY, and leaves the block on arbitrary internet http in
      place. NSAllowsArbitraryLoads would also work and is the wrong tool - it
      turns the protection off everywhere, and App Review asks why.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription
      Since iOS 14 an app must ask before it may contact devices on the local
      network, and the user is shown this sentence verbatim. Without the key
      the app does not get a refusal - it gets silence: every probe times out
      and the shop's own till is invisible while the cloud address works fine.
      Write it as an explanation to a waiter, not to a developer.
-->
<dict>
    <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>

    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Captain looks for this shop's till on the Wi-Fi so orders reach the kitchen instantly, and keep working when the internet does not.</string>

    <!-- Bonjour is what iOS gates local-network access behind. The app sweeps
         addresses rather than browsing services, but iOS still expects a
         declared service type before it will show the permission prompt. -->
    <!--
      The camera, for scanning the shop's code.

      iOS kills an app outright that touches the camera without this key: not
      a refusal the app can handle and explain, a termination. The sentence is
      shown to the user verbatim, so it says why a waiter is being asked.
    -->
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Captain uses the camera to scan the shop's code or photograph a handwritten order for you to review before sending it to the kitchen.</string>

    <!--
      The microphone, for a waiter who says the order instead of tapping it.

      Same class of failure as the camera: iOS terminates an app that reaches
      for the microphone with no declaration. Written to a waiter, because the
      sentence is what they are shown.
    -->
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Captain uses the microphone when you record a voice message for the kitchen or speak an order.</string>

    <!--
      Speech recognition is a SEPARATE permission from the microphone on iOS,
      and asking for one does not ask for the other. Declared because a shop
      may use the handset's own recogniser, which works with no key and with
      the internet down; a shop using a cloud provider records here and
      transcribes on its own server, and never reaches this.
    -->
    <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Captain turns what you say into an order for this shop's menu. Nothing is sent to the kitchen until you have read it and pressed Add.</string>

    <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
    <array>
        <string>_http._tcp</string>
    </array>
</dict>
